tates the raised exception or the predicate gains a leg argument, so the tagging abstraction's shape is justified.","cohort":["A1"],"criteria":["A1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":0.05,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["should_fall_back today is `def should_fall_back(exc: Exception) -> bool:` and classifies `classify_llm_failure(exc)` — it never receives the model","The plan tags the marker in build_fallback_model 'via a small _wrap_fallback_leg WrapperModel' (a MODEL wrapper), then reads it in should_fall_back as an 'early retain-marker check'","No described mechanism threads the model's tag onto the exception the predicate inspects"],"finding":"The plan makes should_fall_back 'leg-aware' via an 'early retain-marker check', and tags the marker on the model in build_fallback_model (_tag_fallback_leg / _wrap_fallback_leg). But should_fall_back's contract receives only the raised EXCEPTION (`def should_fall_back(exc: Exception)`), not the model that raised it. The plan does not specify how a model-level tag becomes visible to a predicate that only sees the exception — leaving the necessity/shape of the four-helper wrapper abstraction unverifiable against the seam it must serve.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f3b508344480ecd81","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Fix plan Change 1 ('leg-aware should_fall_back (early retain-marker check ...)') vs src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py:366-392 (should_fall_back) and 454-483 (build_fallback_model)","norm_id":"na6bb00db95d94ea1","priority":1.0,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Implementer wiring the retain-marker check into should_fall_back and finding the predicate has no handle on which leg raised"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_